name o1-08-belt-netted-1
timeout 900

# CAMPAIGN O1, SORTIE 8 OF 10 - THE STRIKE ON THE BELT, WITH THE BELT'S NET INTACT.
# THIS FILE IS THE HEAD OF THE CAMPAIGN'S CARRY CHAIN. Sortie 10 flies with the aircraft this sortie
# leaves alive, over the belt this sortie leaves standing, on the magazines this sortie leaves loaded.
# CONTROL: none - it IS the control, of o1-09-comms-jam.fbm.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. The Bekaa runs NNE-SSW between the Lebanon and the
# Anti-Lebanon; FlightBox flies it as a parallel at 33.90 N because it has NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4),
# so the two ridges that are the valley's whole geography do not exist in this tree and a mission that
# pretended otherwise would be lying with coordinates. The campaign is flown with `--elev const`, i.e.
# on a 0 m datum: the real valley floor is ~900 m, so every altitude here is height above the DECLARED
# datum and not above the real Bekaa. Rayak is 33.85 N 35.99 E and Damascus/Mezzeh 33.48 N 36.22 E [T4];
# the SAM belt of this campaign stands at 36.20-36.40 E, i.e. the eastern group, between the two.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION, AND ITS DIRECTION — this is the binding disclosure of doc/campaigns/o1-bekaa-1982.md
# and it is repeated in every file of this campaign because a reader who skips it will read the result
# backwards. The MiG-29 was NOT at Bekaa: it entered Soviet service in 1983, and on 9 June 1982 the
# Syrian side flew MiG-21, MiG-23 and Su-20 [T4]. Those aircraft had "only nose and tail alert radar
# systems and no side warnings or look-up and look-down systems" [T4]. FlightBox's MiG-29 has the SPO-15
# with eight azimuth channels, a look-down-capable N019 and an IRST. THE SUBSTITUTION THEREFORE MAKES THE
# DEFENDER STRONGER THAN HISTORY. A FlightBox Bekaa that still ends in a rout says something STRONGER
# than 1982 did; one that ends well for Red says NOTHING about 1982 and must not be read as if it did.
#
# THE GEOMETRY OF A SEAD RUN. Two F-16 ingress from the west at 4 000 m, 152 km out, each carrying two
# Mk 82 and nothing air-to-air at all - the SMS steps through the occupied stations in ascending order
# and weapon selection is not a decision either pilot can make (doc/duels.md D4), so a striker with an
# AIM-9 on station 6 would be a striker that shoots it. 4 000 m is inside the 2K12's 100-7 000 m band,
# inside the S-125's 100-18 000 m band and inside the S-75's 450-25 000 m band: three of the four fire
# units can reach the run-in, and the fourth is the same type as the third. An EF-111 runs a parallel
# leg 66.7 km to the south at 9 000 m and 480 kt.
#
# WHY RED NEVER FLIES OVER ITS OWN BELT, AND IT IS A GAP AND NOT A CHOICE. A FlightBox SAM site has no
# IFF interrogator (`modules/ground/FBSiteModule`: "a battery has no IFF interrogator in this tree") and
# a radar contact carries no identity by construction, so a battery engages the nearest firm track in its
# envelope WHOEVER IT BELONGS TO. Measured on this campaign's own first layout: with the Syrian CAP at
# 8 000 m over the belt, `swsa2` put three V-750 into its own fighters inside 7 s. Every mission of O1 is
# therefore laid out so that no Red aircraft is ever inside a friendly envelope — which DELETES the
# anchor's own Syrian tactic ("do what they can, then run back for cover" inside the protective missile
# envelope [T4]). That is stated here rather than hidden in a gap table: the campaign cannot fly the one
# thing the Syrians actually did.
#
# AND HERE THE BELT FIRES. The strikers run in at 4 000 m, which is inside the 2K12's 100-7 000 m band,
# inside the S-125's 100-18 000 m band and inside the S-75's 450-25 000 m band - three of the four fire
# units can reach them, and the fourth is the same type as the third. Every battery is `emcon hold`
# (dark until something it RECEIVED wakes it) and `autonomy hold` (cut off from the node, it waits), and
# the node transmits `wcs free`. That is one specific, historically real doctrine and the campaign does
# not prefer it: doc/air-defence-network.md section 5 carries `free` and `hold` as the two poles and
# refuses to rank them.
#
# NO AIRCRAFT IN THIS SORTIE DECLARES `objective survive`, AND NO FIGHTER FLIES IN IT AT ALL. Both are
# consequences of one runner property rather than of doctrine: `FBMissionRunner` ENDS THE WHOLE RUN at
# the first flight-monitor K.O. and at the first failed `survive` (`FirstFlightKo` / `FirstDecidingFailure`).
# MEASURED on this campaign's own first layout of this sortie: with four MiG-29 and two escorts in the
# file, the fighter engagement produced its first combat-ineffective jet at t = 169.9 s and that jet hit
# the ground at t = 237.0 s - so every strike sortie ended at t = 237.0 with the strikers still 130 km
# short of the belt and the belt with nothing to shoot at. Putting a fighter duel and a SEAD run in one
# file therefore does not measure the SEAD run; it measures the duel twice. The fighters have their own
# seven sorties, and sorties 08 and 09 are the SEAD pair the operation was actually named after.
# The loss of an aircraft is carried by the campaign regardless of any objective (the carry reads the
# roster's CombatEffective bit), so dropping the line costs the campaign nothing.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: what does an intact, netted, cued air-defence belt do to a SEAD run
# - and what does the run do to it? This is the first sortie of O1 in which the ground fires at all,
# and it is the operation's actual subject: Mole Cricket 19 was a SEAD mission, 30 batteries against
# which 29 were destroyed in about six hours [T4].
#
# WHAT CARRIES OUT OF HERE, and it is `campaign CARRY` lines in sortie 10's own events.log:
#   units   every hawk, eagle and bolt that dies here is DROPPED from sortie 10's roster
#   ground  every battery that dies here is DROPPED - sortie 10 flies over the holes this one made
#   stores  every `set store` line spent here is dropped from sortie 10's spawn
# The belt's own EXPENDED ROUNDS do NOT carry, and that is a gap rather than a decision: the magazine of
# a ground position is `set rounds`, not `set store`, and the campaign layer's store carry is keyed on
# `set store` lines (doc/missions/campaign.md section 4). A battery that shot itself dry here is full
# again in sortie 10.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint cf0e95b1e1f8f910): THE NETTED BELT WORKS EXACTLY AS SPECIFIED, AND DESTROYS
# ITSELF. `net JOIN` on all four fire units at t = 3.9 s; 79 `net CUE`; first firm track at t = 508.5 s
# at 34.9 km on `bksa3` - 13.0 km and 76.6 s further out than the same belt manages unaided (o1-09);
# five `site LAUNCH`, three from the S-125 at t = 539.2 / 541.7 / 572.4 and one each from the two 2K12
# at t = 611.7 / 653.7. NOT ONE ROUND REACHED AN AIRCRAFT. All three V-601 nosed over and hit the
# ground AT THEIR OWN LAUNCHER within 1.6 s of release; both 3M9 did the same, and their 59 kg warheads
# destroyed the batteries that fired them (`damage KILL unit=bksa6a` at t = 612.5, rangeM 0.375;
# `bksa6b` at t = 654.5, rangeM 0.372). What actually killed belt positions was the strike: two Mk 82 on
# `bksa3` and `bksa6a` at t = 664.9 and 684.7, at 22.87 m and 18.41 m.
#
# THE DEFECT IS PRE-EXISTING AND VISIBLE IN A COMMITTED MISSION, so it is reported and not fixed here:
# `net-cue.fbm`, t = 172.8 s - `monitor KO unit=sam_3m9_1 reason=CFIT` 0.8 s after that battery's own
# first launch, followed by `damage KILL unit=sam reason="structure destroyed"` at rangeM 0.0018. The
# 2K12 kills itself with its first round in the tree's own acceptance mission for the net. Fixing it
# means touching `sim/assets/aircraft/`, which this round may not do. It is the single largest reason
# O1's ground half moves no outcome.
#
# READING RULE: exit 1 or 3, both expected; the verdict is `site LAUNCH` / `site STATE` / `net CUE` on
# the belt and `objective kill unit` on the two strikers. Read in this order: `net JOIN` (the belt comes
# up on its net), `net CUE` with its two staleness terms, `site STATE ... to=TRACK/ENGAGE`,
# `site LAUNCH` with `trackToLaunchS`, then who arrived and who did not. exit 2 would be a defect.

zone flak    33.90000 36.30000  9000    0  2500   # under the SAMs, above nothing
zone sa6belt 33.90000 36.30000 24000  100  7000   # the mobile layer
zone sa2belt 33.90000 36.30000 30000  450 25000   # the strategic layer

net iads
  control bkp18                                # the node: a search set and a radio
  link radio 60000 mast 8                       # [SET] 8 m masts -> 23.3 km of horizon. RADIO, because
  period 4.0                                    # a buried cable is unjammable and the anchor's decisive
  hold 3                                        # mechanism was jamming the RADIO net
  wcs free                                      # what the node transmits
  member bkp18
  member bksa6a autonomy hold
  member bksa6b autonomy hold
  member bksa2  autonomy hold
  member bksa3  autonomy hold

unit bolt1
  team friendly
  flight str8 1
  module f16
  spawn 33.90000 34.55000 4000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 80
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode off                            # a striker's radar is not the package's eyes
  set rwr on
  set store 3 mk82                            # a strike element carries no air-to-air round: see header
  set store 7 mk82
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 33.90000 36.20000 4000 450                       # THE TARGET: the active steerpoint IS the aim point
  objective kill unit bksa3

unit bolt2
  team friendly
  flight str8 2
  module f16
  spawn 33.92000 34.55000 4000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 80
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode off                            # a striker's radar is not the package's eyes
  set rwr on
  set store 3 mk82                            # a strike element carries no air-to-air round: see header
  set store 7 mk82
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 33.94000 36.25000 4000 450                       # THE TARGET: the active steerpoint IS the aim point
  objective kill unit bksa6a


unit boeing
  team friendly
  module ef111                                # the Boeing 707 ECM aircraft's stand-in: a catalogue row
  spawn 33.30000 34.90000 9000 90.0 480       # whose ONLY weapon is `jam_comm_m` (doc/modules/air)
  set drag_threat_s 0                         # ordered to hold the leg: the honest way to say "never runs"
  set jam_comm_m 0
  wp 33.30000 36.60000 9000 480                # three legs, so the leg it is on never runs out: a mover
  wp 33.60000 35.60000 9000 480                # that reaches its last waypoint enters an ORBIT and
  wp 33.30000 36.60000 9000 480                # drifts, and a drifting jammer un-jams (measured: the

unit bkp18
  module p18                                  # P-18 "Spoon Rest D": the net's control node - the
  team hostile                                # LEADERSHIP the Israeli plan attacked, and it is killable
  spawn 33.90000 36.30000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon free

unit bksa6a
  module sa6                                  # 2K12 Kub - the mobile layer, the type that made Bekaa
  team hostile
  spawn 33.94000 36.25000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold                              # dark until something it RECEIVED says otherwise
  set rounds 3

unit bksa6b
  module sa6
  team hostile
  spawn 33.86000 36.35000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 3

unit bksa2
  module sa2                                  # S-75 - the strategic layer, 450-25 000 m
  team hostile
  spawn 33.90000 36.40000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 3

unit bksa3
  module sa3                                  # S-125 - the low/medium layer, 100-18 000 m
  team hostile
  spawn 33.90000 36.20000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 3

unit bkhq
  module target_hard                          # the site command post - a hardened aim point [SET]
  team hostile
  spawn 33.90500 36.30500 ground 0.0 0
